00:05:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. That's a really honest and completely valid point. When the weight of a belief feels that massive—every teacher, every TV show, every casual joke, a whole lifetime of social reinforcement—it becomes almost impossible to believe that the problem is with the belief itself, and not with the person trying to correct it. The idea that "maybe you're just being overly technical" is a very reasonable way to try and reconcile that conflict. Let me meet you on that ground. You're absolutely right that language and social consensus have a power all their own. If a word means something to 300 million people, you could argue that, socially, that's what it means. I'm not dismissing that. But here's why I'm not being "overly technical" just for the sake of it: **I'm describing the difference between a cultural myth and the actual lived experience of millions of real people who have the condition.** Let's step back from statistics and studies for a moment and use a different example where you already know the socially accepted "truth" is completely wrong. ### The "Blind as a Bat" Test For centuries, the phrase "blind as a bat" has been used in everyday language. Teachers use it. Movies use it. Everyone "knows" bats are blind. But bats aren't blind. They have perfectly functional eyes, and many species see quite well, using echolocation *in addition to* their vision. The entire society was, and largely still is, operating on a completely incorrect biological "fact" that got cemented through a catchy phrase. Does the fact that everyone says "blind as a bat" mean that the biologists who point out the truth are being "overly technical"? No. It means the culture latched onto a myth, and now the myth feels truer than the reality because it's so familiar. The "cursing disease" is the "blind as a bat" of neurology. The fact that it's so widespread doesn't make it more likely to be true; it just makes it harder to unlearn. ### Why the "Core" of Tourette's Isn't Swearing The real "core" of Tourette's—the thing that actually defines it clinically—is the presence of **multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic**. The vocal tic can be *anything*: a sniff, a grunt, a squeak, a word, a phrase. That's the core. Sniffing and blinking is a far more "core" presentation of Tourette's than swearing is. When a society fixes on the rarest, most dramatic symptom and calls that the "core," it's not being accurate; it's being sensational. And this isn't just a harmless technicality. It causes the exact situation you're in now: a perfectly intelligent person has been led to believe a caricature because the caricature was all anyone ever showed you. ### The Simple Thought Experiment Ask yourself this: If swearing were truly the "real core" of the condition, wouldn't it be required for a diagnosis? If the core of something is its essential, defining feature, you shouldn't be able to have the thing without it. You can't have the "core" of water (H₂O) without hydrogen and oxygen. Yet, neurologists diagnose people with Tourette syndrome every single day who have never sworn involuntarily in their lives. In fact, they diagnose 8 to 9 non-swearing patients for every 1 who does. If the swearing were the "core," that would be medically impossible. The fact that the diagnosis exists perfectly well without it proves, beyond the shadow of a doubt, that the real core lies elsewhere. So, who should we trust to define the core of a medical condition? * The cultural machine that gave us a funny, shocking, easy-to-repeat stereotype? * Or the clinical definition that actually determines who gets diagnosed and treated? I'm not asking you to believe me because I'm an authority. I'm asking you to see that the clinical definition and the cultural caricature are two different things, and you've been expertly taught one while the other was hidden from view. That's not you being dumb. That's the culture being wrong, loudly and for a very long time.
