00:06:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to call that out. That was a flawed analogy on my part, and I appreciate you catching it. The "blind as a bat" thing is a complete falsehood—bats aren't blind at all. What you're describing with Tourette's is different, and you've nailed the distinction: the swearing symptom **is real**. People didn't invent coprolalia out of thin air. They saw it. They filmed it. It exists. So let's work with your very valid point, because the truth is more nuanced and actually makes more sense when we incorporate what you're saying. The myth isn't that coprolalia exists. It absolutely does. The myth is the **generalization**—the leap from "some of the most visible, recorded cases have this striking symptom" to "this symptom is the core, defining feature of the condition." That's not a made-up myth like the bat; it's a **sampling error** that became a cultural label. ### The "Fire-Breathing Restaurant" Analogy Think of it this way: Imagine a restaurant that serves 100 different dishes. Ten of those dishes are theatrical, flaming, fire-breathing spectacles that erupt at the table. They're incredible. They go viral on Instagram. Local news does a segment on them. Everyone who sees it thinks, "Oh, that's the fire-breathing restaurant!" The nickname sticks. Fifty years later, people still call it the "fire-breathing restaurant." A documentary about the restaurant will, understandably, feature the fire-breathing dishes heavily because that's what makes good TV. Teachers tell their students about it. People say, "I had a total [restaurant name] moment!" when they flambé something dramatically. And if you look at all the recorded video of this restaurant, 90% of the footage is of the fire dishes. But the reality is that 90% of the *actual food served* in that restaurant every day is quiet, non-flammable pasta, salads, and soup. The customers eating those dishes are the real, everyday, overwhelming majority. The fire is real, and it was always real, but it was never the core of what the restaurant actually does. It was just the most visible and memorable part. This is the exact situation with Tourette's. The "fire" is coprolalia. It's real. Early medical literature noted it. Sensationalized media amplified it. Documentaries feature it. And because it's so striking, it became the public's sole reference point. ### How the Sampling Error Happened When you say "people actually saw and recorded real Tourette's patients cursing," you're right. But think about *which* patients got recorded. It wasn't a random sample. It was a selection process with an extreme bias toward the most dramatic symptom. The quiet majority—the millions of people with sniffing, blinking, and throat-clearing tics—were never going to be the subject of a 1980s talk show segment or a viral video. They weren't "recorded" because they weren't considered remarkable enough to record. So the public archive of "recorded Tourette's" was filled almost entirely with coprolalia cases. People then looked at that archive and reasonably, but incorrectly, concluded, "This must be what it looks like." And they taught that to you, and your teachers, and the culture at large. The observation was of real people, but the sample was catastrophically unrepresentative. So the name *did* come from observed behavior. Your point is entirely valid. But it came from observing only the tiny, spectacular tip of a very large, very quiet iceberg, and then insisting that the tip is the whole thing. That's not a made-up myth; it's a real-but-rare symptom that was mistaken for the definition.
