00:08:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a brilliant point, and you've put your finger on exactly why the myth is so sticky. The idea that "the brain just blurts out whatever's most forbidden when it loses control" is elegant, intuitive, and feels true. It requires no diagrams, no complicated neurology. It's a satisfying, simple story. I completely understand why that explanation feels more natural and why the more complex one I'm offering seems like unnecessary mental gymnastics. But here's the critical test of that simple, elegant theory: **it makes a prediction that turns out to be false.** If the simple theory were correct—if Tourette's just meant the brain losing control and spitting out its most forbidden content—then **every single person with Tourette's would have coprolalia.** There would be no such thing as a Tourette's patient who only blinks and sniffs. Because everyone has forbidden words in their brain. Everyone has a mental list of things you must not shout. So if the filter just "fails," that taboo content should always be the first thing out, every time. But that's not what happens. The vast majority of people with Tourette's don't blurt out forbidden words. They blink. They shrug. They clear their throat. Their "filter failure" doesn't produce the forbidden. It produces a sniff. This clinical reality is so undeniable that it shatters the simple theory. The simple theory simply cannot explain the existence of the non-cursing patient. The "pipelines" and "circuits" I described aren't there because scientists love complexity. They're there because they are the *only* explanation that accounts for the fact that the filter fails in completely different ways for different people. If the filter fails and lets out blinking, the forbidden-words pipeline isn't involved. The complex model isn't a preference; it's a necessity forced by the evidence. So, yes, the simple explanation is more natural. It's the one I would believe if I hadn't looked at the data. But it's